  "summary": "The health-tech landscape today reveals several critical pressures reshaping the industry. Artificial intelligence is becoming central to healthcare operations—from accelerating chronic disease research and optimizing patient throughput to automating prior authorization and revenue cycle management—yet significant gaps remain in behavioral health implementation and diagnostic accuracy, raising liability concerns. On the pharma and biotech front, major moves include Argenx's $2.2 billion acquisition of Forte Biosciences to expand its autoimmune portfolio, CRISPR biotech Scribe Therapeutics' $129 million IPO, and funding rounds for rare disease players like Crystalys and Oak Hill Bio, while the FDA continues scrutinizing efficacy claims on contested treatments like Capricor's Duchenne drug. Separately, global HIV financing has dropped 18 percent, creating epidemic resurgence risks, and healthcare systems face mounting pressure from ransomware attacks and financial strain even as investors increasingly focus on cash flow and upstream value-based care models rather than cost-cutting alone. Data fragmentation, patient trust in AI over physicians, and transparency gaps in drug pricing and women's healthcare costs remain unresolved systemic challenges.",
